<h2 class="mt-14">Consent and purchaser suppression for Order tracking</h2>
<p>Separate necessary shipment and exception notices from marketing. Record purpose and channel eligibility, current order and carrier state, and each sender’s responsibilities. Purchase exits cart recovery, not the tracking service the buyer needs. Pause conflicting promotions during unresolved cases; separately eligible care and education can continue.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Audience state</th><th>Required handling</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>No documented consent</td><td>Hold marketing without documented eligibility; assess necessary order notices separately</td><td>An order authorizes no blanket marketing permission; service and marketing have different eligibility</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consented, never purchased</td><td>Offer general delivery guidance only to eligible subscribers; no order-specific tracking without an order</td><td>A subscriber record does not establish shipment or carrier state</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active cart, no checkout</td><td>Keep recovery separate from tracking; verify recovery permission and current purchase state</td><td>A cart is not a shipment and does not authorize a tracking notice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purchased recently</td><td>Exit cart recovery; continue accurate necessary tracking and separately eligible care</td><td>Purchase should start relevant service, not suppress it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refund or return open</td><td>Pause conflicting promotions during cases; retain necessary shipment, return, and refund updates</td><td>Case closure does not restore withdrawn permission or clear other suppressions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repeated non-engagement</td><td>Assess clicks, delivery, orders, and recency before sunsetting marketing</td><td>Non-opening alone is not a reason to withhold necessary shipment updates</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMS consent present</td><td>Verify purpose-specific SMS eligibility, opt-outs, quiet hours, and frequency caps</td><td>Email marketing permission does not establish SMS permission</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wholesale or B2B account</td><td>Send order tracking B2B accounts down their own track</td><td>Check account terms and recipient eligibility; B2B status is not permission</td></tr>
<tr><td>Free or disposable email domain</td><td>Review actual delivery and abuse signals; distinguish free providers from disposable addresses</td><td>A free email domain alone is not grounds to suppress tracking</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staff and test accounts</td><td>Exclude staff and test profiles from live sends and production reporting</td><td>Order Tracking reporting stays clean without test noise</td></tr>
<tr><td>Competitor or researcher signals</td><td>Order Tracking researcher signups follow standard consent</td><td>Ad-hoc order tracking exceptions break traceability</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legacy list without timestamps</td><td>Hold uncertain marketing records while checking permission evidence and applicable requirements</td><td>Do not send re-permission campaigns to records ineligible for marketing</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="mt-12">Margin, app costs, and pricing for Order tracking</h2>
<p>Judge order tracking on margin, not attribution. A durable order tracking program absorbs subscription, overages, SMS, capture, creative, agency, and discount costs and still pays. If order tracking tooling eats toward fifteen percent of attributed margin, reduce order tracking scope before optimizing flows.</p>
<p>Pricing changes frequently and varies by region, contact volume, and contract term, so check the official pricing pages of every shortlisted app and model an eighteen-month total that includes a peak season. Order Tracking free tiers look generous until one limit bites — verify which of contacts, sends, branching, or support constrains order tracking first.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table>
